  "summary": "Agent-level generative emulators, hybrid ODE-mixture models, and variance-decomposition tools make stochastic disease models faster to run and easier to dissect.",
  "synthesis": "The three items share a common concern with heterogeneity and stochasticity in disease simulation, approached from different directions. A preprint applies conditional tabular GANs to emulate road-injury agents within a multi-disease agent-based model, reporting an 86.6% reduction in run-time while preserving dynamics. A second preprint introduces LCPTA, a hybrid ODE and latent class trajectory framework implemented in the TemporalMixtureModels.jl Julia package, for modeling multiscale heterogeneous disease progression. A published study applies uncertainty quantification tools to separate noise channels in stochastic epidemic models, illustrated with a yellow fever virus spillover model in the Americas. What is new across these is the use of learned or mixture-based components to represent agent- or subpopulation-level variation alongside formal attribution of stochastic variability.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
